    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but I'm still having a hard time visualizing how they \"work together\" in real-time. It feels like two different explanations for the same thing. Is there a way to think about the relationship between the air pressure and that downward deflection so it doesn't just feel like two separate processes?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Think of it as two sides of the same coin of air movement. The shape of the wing causes the air to accelerate over the top (Bernoulli), creating a *pressure differential*. This pressure differential, in turn, forces the air downward—this downward momentum is the physical manifestation of the force described by Newton's third law. So, the pressure difference creates the required downward deflection, which is the \"action\" that results in the upward \"reaction\" (lift).",
      "source": "target",
